    "chunk_size_bytes": 1840,
    "chunk_text": "Three types of cardiac stents withdrawn immediately due to safety concerns\n\nColombo, June 19 (Daily Mirror) - The Medical Supplies Division (MSD) have ordered the immediate withdrawal of three types of 'inferior' cardiac stents and instructed hospitals across Sri Lanka to isolate all remaining stocks without using them, in a major nationwide safety alert affecting heart treatment services.\n\nThe directive comes as health authorities move swiftly to prevent potential risks to patients undergoing angioplasty procedures, after regulatory reviews found serious concerns regarding the performance and safety of the devices in clinical use. Hospitals have been told to urgently identify affected stocks, remove them from circulation, and ensure they are not used under any circumstances.\n\nThe decision involves coronary stents that are widely used to treat blocked arteries and improve blood flow in heart patients. According to health sector officials, the withdrawal is a precautionary but urgent measure aimed at protecting patient safety following warnings raised through regulatory assessments and expert evaluations.\n\nUnder the instructions issued by the National Medicines Regulatory Authority (NMRA), all Provincial Health Directors, Hospital Directors, and Heads of Medical Institutions have been directed to strictly enforce the recall. Remaining stocks of the affected stents must be set aside immediately and reported, while usage has been completely prohibited pending further investigation.\n\nThe MSD have highlighted that the move is necessary due to potential risks associated with the devices, including complications that could arise during or after cardiac procedures. Medical professionals have been advised to closely monitor patients who have already received the stents, as part of ongoing clinical safety surveillance."
  },
