    "chunk_size_bytes": 1787,
    "chunk_text": "The G7 has always carried this tension between appearance and reality. It presents itself as a gathering of democratic partners working for global stability, but its origins were far more exclusive. It began not as a universal institution but as a private conversation among powerful states facing economic crisis. In the early 1970s, after the collapse of the Bretton Woods monetary system and the oil shock that shook industrial economies, finance ministers and central bankers from leading economies began informal discussions known as the Library Group. The name itself reflected the quiet nature of the meetings. They were not public assemblies. They were conversations among officials who believed they had the economic weight to solve problems affecting the wider world.\n\nFor years, critics from outside the group described it as an elite club. The criticism was not without foundation. Countries across Africa, Asia and Latin America often argued that decisions affecting global trade, finance and development were being shaped by nations that represented only part of humanity. The G7 never claimed to be the United Nations. It was designed for influence rather than representation.\n\nThat was precisely why it mattered. When the United States, Japan and major European economies moved together, markets listened. Governments adjusted. International institutions followed. The power of the G7 came from concentration.\n\nThe world that produced the G7 no longer exists. China became an industrial giant. India became a major economic force. Middle powers gained greater diplomatic independence. Global supply chains spread power beyond the traditional Western centres. Yet the G7 remained built around the assumption that its members were still the main architects of global order."
  },
  {
