    "md5": "143d28fd8a9486b9e7deb28c04900531",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1887,
    "chunk_text": "The world that produced the G7 no longer exists. China became an industrial giant. India became a major economic force. Middle powers gained greater diplomatic independence. Global supply chains spread power beyond the traditional Western centres. Yet the G7 remained built around the assumption that its members were still the main architects of global order.\n\nThe other G7 leaders understood that confrontation with Trump carried risks. Europe\u2019s security dependence on the United States remains substantial. Intelligence cooperation, military support for Ukraine and NATO\u2019s deterrence capability all rely heavily on American involvement. The polite atmosphere around Trump was not simply respect for a visiting president. It was a calculation by governments aware that the alliance could not easily absorb another major internal conflict.\n\nThe summit therefore became an exercise in political management. Climate change, traditionally one of the major pillars of G7 discussions, received less attention. The agenda moved towards issues where Washington and its partners could find common ground, particularly China\u2019s economic practices, critical minerals and industrial competition. Diplomacy became less about presenting shared ideals and more about avoiding unnecessary fractures.\n\nThe dispute over Iran showed the same pattern. Trump promoted the agreement as a breakthrough, while critics argued that the arrangement offered Tehran a significant economic lifeline. The framework reportedly included sanctions relief mechanisms, renewed oil exports, access to frozen funds and discussions around a major reconstruction programme. Supporters argued that restrictions on Iran\u2019s nuclear ambitions represented progress. Opponents argued that the deal rewarded a government that had survived years of international pressure without fundamentally changing its strategic behaviour."
  },
