    "chunk_text": "Institutional investors, falling oil prices help boost stock trading\n\nThe CSE yesterday picked up with some significant participation by institutional investors. Factors that helped in the process were impressive export earnings and falling world oil prices, market analysts said.\n\nAmid those developments both indices moved upwards. The All Share Price Index was up by 198.46 points, while the S and P SL20 rose by 73.9 points.\n\nTurnover stood at Rs 2.73 billion with seven crossings. Those crossings were : Commercial Bank crossed 219,680 shares to the tune of Rs 46 million, its shares traded at Rs 209, Singer Sri Lanka 250,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 40 million; its shares traded at Rs 77, HNB (Non-Voting) 100,000 shares crossed for Rs 33.7 million; its shares sold at Rs 337, CIC Holdings 750,000 shares crossed tfor Rs 24 million; its share s sold at Rs 32.50.\n\nTokyo Cement (Non-Voting) 360,000 shares crossed for Rs 24 million; its shares traded at Rs 56, Tokyo Cement 265,000 shares crossed for Rs 23 million; its shares sold at Rs 87.30 and Aitken Spence 130,000 shares crossed for Rs 21 million; its shares fetched Rs 158.\n\nIn the retail market top seven companies that mainly contributed to the turnover were; Commercial Credit Rs 197 million (1.6 million shares traded), Digital Mobility Solutions Rs 172 million (992,000 shares traded), Samson International Rs 116 million (714,000 ,shares traded), Dipped Products Rs 90 million (1.5 million shares traded), Royal Ceramics Rs 81 million (1.7 million shares traded) and Hayleys Rs 73 million (306,000 shares traded). During the day 67.8 million share volumes changed hands in 19136 transactions.\n\nIt is said that the banking and financial sectors performed well, especially Commercial Bank, while construction sector counters, especially Tokyo Cement, performed well too. The manufacturing sector, especially Haycarb and Dipped Products, were impressive."
