    "md5": "693a912a531cc8e2e39671508839e3ed",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1708,
    "chunk_text": "By May 2026, the scope had widened. Proposals on the table now include prohibiting children under five from accessing digital screens entirely, alongside the under-12 social media ban. The Deputy Minister of Digital Economy, Eranga Weeraratne, confirmed that ministerial-level discussions had begun.\n\nIf passed, the bill would give Sri Lanka its first dedicated legal framework for a minimum social media age, putting it in the same bracket as Australia, the UK, France, and other countries already moving on this front. It is worth noting that a Private Member's Bill is not the same as a government bill: it still needs to clear procedural hurdles and parliamentary debate before it has any chance of becoming law, and it remains to be seen how it will be reconciled with the lower, under-12 threshold the government's own ministerial discussions have been built around. But its mere existence signals that the political appetite for legislating on this question, rather than simply discussing it, is now real.\n\nThis is still, in large part, at the discussion stage. Any formal policy will require cabinet approval, a legislative framework, and a technical enforcement mechanism. Officials have said they are studying international models, with Australia's approach drawing the most attention. The government has also indicated that local telecom companies may be asked to play a role in implementing age verification.\n\nSri Lanka's Online Safety Act, No. 9 of 2024, currently provides the only legislative instrument available to regulate online communication in the country. It was not designed with children's social media use specifically in mind, which is why advocates are pushing for a dedicated law."
