    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4f71d619cc3f4a15f731f674c2d997bd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1985,
    "chunk_text": "Meat, morals, and the carbon footprint\n\nRecently, a Buddhist monk whom I deeply respect for his understanding of the Buddha\u2019s teaching stated with absolute conviction that there is nothing wrong with eating meat. He argued that no rule binds the laity in this matter and that meat consumption is not an unwholesome act, or papa karma. If his point was that eating meat has no proven consequence for a future life, I can accept that position, because no empirical evidence can settle such a claim.\n\nBut karma is not merely a theory about the afterlife; at its heart, it is a discipline of cause and effect. Seen in that light, it is difficult to claim that eating meat has no consequence at all. Whatever one may believe about future lives, and regardless of religious affiliation, the effects of meat consumption are visible here and now: in the climate, the land, public health, animal suffering, and the moral choices we make.\n\nAnimal farming is often discussed as a matter of food, culture, or personal preference. Yet when we look more closely, it is also a matter of consequence. Every system of production creates effects, and animal agriculture creates effects that reach far beyond the dinner plate. It affects the climate, land, water, public health, the lives of animals, and the moral habits of human beings. Whether one speaks in religious, ethical, or scientific language, the principle is the same: actions have results, and meat consumption is no exception.\n\nOne of the most visible consequences of animal farming is environmental damage. Livestock systems produce greenhouse gases, including methane, nitrous oxide, and carbon dioxide. Methane from cattle digestion and manure is especially powerful in warming the atmosphere. In this sense, meat production is not merely a private act of consumption; it is part of a global pattern that contributes to climate change.\n\nChart: Greenhouse Gas Emissions, Measured as Kilograms of CO2-Equivalent per 100 Grams of Protein"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-06-27-islandlk-56f9b404",
    "num": "islandlk-56f9b404",
    "date_str": "2026-06-27",
    "description": "Meat, morals, and the carbon footprint",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/meat-morals-and-the-carbon-footprint/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1782576960.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-06-27-islandlk-56f9b404-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7920330d59f94ae0a158c24b4894e104",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "One of the most visible consequences of animal farming is environmental damage. Livestock systems produce greenhouse gases, including methane, nitrous oxide, and carbon dioxide. Methane from cattle digestion and manure is especially powerful in warming the atmosphere. In this sense, meat production is not merely a private act of consumption; it is part of a global pattern that contributes to climate change.\n\nChart: Greenhouse Gas Emissions, Measured as Kilograms of CO2-Equivalent per 100 Grams of Protein\n\nBy comparison, many plant proteins produce only a small fraction of that footprint. Recent estimates also place livestock emissions at roughly 12% of global human-caused greenhouse gas emissions, while the wider agrifood system accounts for about one-third. For that reason, one practical contribution individuals can make is not necessarily to become vegetarian overnight, but to reduce high-impact meat consumption and choose lower-carbon sources of protein whenever possible.\n\nThe consequences of animal farming are also found in water and soil. Large farms produce substantial amounts of manure, and when this waste is poorly managed, it can contaminate rivers, lakes, and groundwater. Nutrients from manure and fertilizer can flow into waterways, causing algal blooms and oxygen-depleted zones where aquatic life cannot survive. Water is also used directly for animals and indirectly for growing their feed. When the full chain of production is considered, many animal products require far more water and land than plant-based foods.\n\nA significant share of the world\u2019s grain supply is also directed to animal feed rather than direct human consumption, making animal agriculture an inefficient way to convert land, water, and crops into calories and protein. Livestock production uses large areas of agricultural land, including pasture and land for feed crops, and continued expansion can contribute to deforestation and habitat loss."
