    "chunk_size_bytes": 1491,
    "chunk_text": "A significant share of the world\u2019s grain supply is also directed to animal feed rather than direct human consumption, making animal agriculture an inefficient way to convert land, water, and crops into calories and protein. Livestock production uses large areas of agricultural land, including pasture and land for feed crops, and continued expansion can contribute to deforestation and habitat loss.\n\nAs an island nation, Sri Lanka has access to naturally sourced or wild-harvested fish and seafood; the carbon emissions associated with this process are generally limited to fishing boats, refrigeration, and transportation. Taken together, these impacts are usually lower than those associated with farmed or imported fish. Yet national fish consumption has reportedly declined in recent years, largely because of inadequate refrigeration and preservation, both at sea and on land, and weak inland distribution infrastructure, which raises the costs and limits access.\n\nBeyond the environment and public health lies the direct suffering of animals. Modern farming often treats living beings as units of production. Animals may be confined, separated from natural behaviors, transported long distances, and slaughtered at a fraction of their natural lifespan. Even if one argues that eating meat is not forbidden by religious rule, the question remains whether unnecessary suffering can be ignored. Compassion asks us to look not only at the final product, but also at the life behind it."
  },
