    "chunk_text": "Since Halifax was a small city, I could see most of the important places within a few days. Halifax is famous for its cold water lobster available year-round and we had lobster dinners most nights. The city was covered with several feet of snow and it was indeed a white Christmas and we took part in the celebrations which included sledding and playing ice sports. Mavis\u2019 friends joined us in these activities which were a great delight. We sang Christmas songs, joked, and reminisced about old friends in Toronto, particularly Siva, Surjeet, Liz, and Anette.\n\nWe spent Christmas in Jim\u2019s parents home with his siblings and family. They were Anglicans while Jim\u2019s wife Roberta was of Italian Catholic descent. At first his parents were not supportive of their marriage on religious grounds but the disapproval faded after their first child was born. Mavis\u2019 older sister was married to an African-American and they had two adopted daughters.\n\nThe women were busy in the kitchen preparing various Christmas dishes, including black pudding, a kind of blood sausage originating in the UK and Ireland, while the men enjoyed beer, snacks, and played card games, darts, and carom. Jim\u2019s younger brother was in the 12th grade and was very friendly with me. He invited me to join him on a hunting trip. I hesitated at first saying I did not like to kill animals, but eventually joined him. He reassured me that we would only shoot at targets, not animals. The hunting experience turned out to be enjoyable and a great physical exercise too.\n\nWhen we returned from the hunting trip we were quite exhausted and hungry. We took a bath, dressed in clean clothes, and went to the table for dinner. Before eating, the responsibility of saying grace to thank God for the food on the table fell on Mavis. Stealing a line from a play she had seen in Halifax, Mavis loudly exclaimed, \u201crub-a-dub-dub, thanks for the grub,\u201d making everyone burst into laughter."
