    "chunk_size_bytes": 1747,
    "chunk_text": "When we returned from the hunting trip we were quite exhausted and hungry. We took a bath, dressed in clean clothes, and went to the table for dinner. Before eating, the responsibility of saying grace to thank God for the food on the table fell on Mavis. Stealing a line from a play she had seen in Halifax, Mavis loudly exclaimed, \u201crub-a-dub-dub, thanks for the grub,\u201d making everyone burst into laughter.\n\nThe next day, it was time to open gifts. I had brought small gifts for Mavis and Jim but I received many extravagant presents from everyone in the family. There wasn\u2019t enough space in my bags to carry all of that after parting with them the following day. I headed back to Pittsburgh with a sense of joy and sorrow as Mavis had high expectations of me. However, as the saying goes, long-distance relationships seldom find their way.\n\nMy PhD research focused on the dynamics and control of automated guideway transit (AGT) systems. These vehicles are completely automated, and hence driverless. They run on elevated guideways and don\u2019t interfere with other traffic. So, AGT had been an attractive area of research at the time.\n\nRobotics was emerging at the same time yet was not properly recognized. I wanted to enter the field but it was clear to me that such research has to be in collaboration with mechanical and electrical engineers and computer scientists. So I was somewhat hesitant. Fortunately, Raj Reddy, a professor of Computer Science at CMU, recognized the importance of robotics and needed interdepartmental collaboration. He contacted some of us who had the right research background in the departments of mechanical and electrical engineering and computer science to work together in robotics. We were quite excited."
  },
