    "md5": "8594c68d6624b37b92a42edf7c42ef79",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1512,
    "chunk_text": "Robotics was emerging at the same time yet was not properly recognized. I wanted to enter the field but it was clear to me that such research has to be in collaboration with mechanical and electrical engineers and computer scientists. So I was somewhat hesitant. Fortunately, Raj Reddy, a professor of Computer Science at CMU, recognized the importance of robotics and needed interdepartmental collaboration. He contacted some of us who had the right research background in the departments of mechanical and electrical engineering and computer science to work together in robotics. We were quite excited.\n\nWestinghouse in Pittsburgh gave us two small commuter planes, single-engine and twin-engine Cessna aircraft, for the trip. Raj asked three of us to take the small plane and leave first and the remaining five including himself would follow in the bigger plane. We landed in Winston Salem and waited for the other plane but it didn\u2019t arrive. There were no cellphones in those days. So, we had to find a land phone somewhere and make a call to CMU. That plane had lost an engine en route and had to return, so the other group was still waiting for a replacement plane from Westinghouse.\n\nEventually everyone arrived, made an effective presentation to the Westinghouse executives and the company committed itself to fund the Robotics Institute at CMU. This was the first such institute anywhere and we grew steadily becoming world-renowned and even started granting CMU PhDs in Robotics. The rest is history!"
  },
  {
