    "md5": "f60e239e49ce4400faa9dc9e2edd9bec",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1574,
    "chunk_text": "Eventually everyone arrived, made an effective presentation to the Westinghouse executives and the company committed itself to fund the Robotics Institute at CMU. This was the first such institute anywhere and we grew steadily becoming world-renowned and even started granting CMU PhDs in Robotics. The rest is history!\n\nFortunately, some of them have returned to Sri Lanka and are carrying out good research and development. My students who have returned to Sri Lanka include Lalith Gamage, who is now a Professor in Sri Lanka and heads Sri Lanka Institute of Information Technology (SLIIT) and Nalin Wickramarachchi who served as Dean of Engineering at UM.\n\nSome general applications of robotics are equally important to developing countries as well as more advanced states. However, we should not blindly decide on what we do in developing countries just for the sake of being involved in robotics or AI. We must explore and determine what is in the \u201cblack box.\u201d (In robotics, \u201cblack box\u201d typically refers to two distinct concepts: opaque AI algorithms whose internal decision-making is hidden, or physical \u201cflight recorders\u201d designed to log data and investigate accidents\n\nOtherwise we can be dissuaded through fear of the way ahead or make wrong choices for robotic activities. We must first question whether robotics is needed for a specific application and then explore what approaches are relevant for the task in hand. Very importantly, we must examine what is in the existing black box and what is already available before implementing new methodology."
