    "md5": "e15de97e06dae0e15b7ba3ce79cb4c39",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1888,
    "chunk_text": "Mangrove restoration seen as creating jobs and promoting rural economic vibrancy\n\nMangrove restoration is emerging as a powerful rural economic development model, generating income, strengthening community enterprises, creating village savings schemes and opening new opportunities in nature-based tourism, according to leading marine scientist Professor Sevvandi Jayakody.\n\nSpeaking to The Island Financial Review, Professor Jayakody said ecosystem restoration should no longer be viewed solely as an environmental exercise but as a long-term investment that delivers measurable economic and social returns while safeguarding Sri Lanka\u2019s natural capital.\n\n\u201cRestoration is helping the community in multiple ways. Let us go into the finances first, because money matters to everyone, Professor Jayakody said.\n\nUsing the Anawilundawa mangrove restoration initiative as an example, she explained that every visitor to the area contributes directly to the local economy. Community members prepare meals, provide services and receive income, while part of the earnings is channeled into a community-managed revolving fund.\n\n\u201cLike you are here today, and you enjoyed their food. That is an income for the community. It also builds community pride because everyone loves to see visitors enjoying what they have to offer, she said.\n\nBeyond immediate earnings, the project has become an engine for rural entrepreneurship. A percentage of every payment made by visitors is saved collectively, enabling villagers to build a community fund that finances small loans, purchases equipment and supports local enterprises.\n\n\u201cEvery time we have visitors, the community provides the food and saves part of the income. That has now become their community fund. They are using it to provide loans to members, buy equipment, from cutlery to pottery, and organise themselves much better, she explained."
